Date: Tue, 9 Feb 93 11:18:39 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: Econ: Zero-sum games

[It seems all my outgoing mail yesterday disappeared (not even cc-ed
back to me), so I am trying to recompose all I had sent. RH]

Perry writes: 

>Maybe I should take a different tack to explain my point, and avoid
>the term "zero sum" for a while.  ... in the derivatives markets
>short positions are half the game! ... Perhaps this is all just a
>semantic debate, but I'm trying to make a very real point here -- if
>you make an uncovered trade in the futures market, you are making a
>gambling bet ...  This is borne out, by the way, by the fact that most
>amateur futures traders, in industry parlance "blow up", that is, lose
>all their investment, and usually very fast.

I think I may be able to offer a restated position that Perry and I
(and ddf, Hal, and the rest of you) can agree to.

Let us assume the standard CAPM finance model.  Then if we take the
proverbial "riskless asset" (return certain relative to ~ cost of
living) as our unit of account, then the average return on any asset
(relative to riskless) is proportional to the covariance of that asset
with the market asset (~ sum of all liquid assets), also called the
asset's "beta".  (Covariance of market with itself is one, so ave
asset return is beta times market return.)

Holding high beta stocks is called a high risk portfolio, holding zero
beta stocks is opting out of the game, and holding a negative beta
portfolio is betting the market will get worse.  The most basic advise
to offer the naive is to avoid betting against progress; avoid a low
beta portfolio.  "Go long young man."  Even if you're right you won't
get much, but if you bet on progress and you're right, you can get
very rich.  (A rather extropian lesson, IMHO.) (Btw, you don't want as
high a beta as possible, since what you really want is high average
log return.)

The beta of a basket of two assets is the average or the beta of each,
and so return is also an average.  Inversely, if one takes an
asset an splits it into two parts, such as splitting a bond into a
continuing and a final payment, the average of the betas of the parts
is the beta of the original asset.  

A short sale can be thought of as splitting a riskless asset into
another copy of an asset, and the inverse of that asset, a short.  The
short will then have a beta the negative of the beta of the asset,
implying a high negative average return if the original was high
positive beta.  Too much of that in your portfolio, and you're betting
against progress!  Bad news.  Of course just a little is fine if you
think you know better than market expectations about that asset.

Now a bet is basically a way to split an asset into two or more state
contingent assets.  So if you bet by splitting a riskless asset, the
same warning applies.  You may be implicitly betting against progress.
But bets need not split riskless assets - they can instead split as
high a beta asset is liquidly available.  In general derivative assets
need not have low average beta.  

Derivates and bets aren't the problem, low average (log) return is.
Instead of warning your children never to go near liquor stores, warn
them against misusing liquor.  Overly-broad warnings, state-enforced
or not, can do great harm.

Hal writes:
>I do see a problem with this, though.  It seems that the total return
>can't _quite_ be as high as that of the financial instrument, since the
>betting exchange has to take a cut.  So the return will be a little less.
>And the risk seems somewhat, possibly quite a bit, greater than the
>risk of just holding the instrument - after all, there may well be a
>better than 50-50 chance that you will lose all of your investment.

Yes transaction costs can lower average returns of derivatives
relative to originals.  In theory, the only risk that the market
will/should pay you for is beta.  The rest you can eliminate with
enough diversification.  Diversify your bets.

