I don't remember Benson's book dealing very much with the notion of
proprietary communities. I believe that the "proprietary communities" meme
was injected into the Extropian legal meme complex by Simon! Levy (although
I might be wrong.)

I don't have "proof" that private communities would remain small, but
I do have two pretty good indicative arguments, which also subsume
the question of how fascist private communities tend to get.

1. Existing communities
Private communities are NOT a new concept. They exist today. They are typically
cooperative apartment buildings, condominium associations, "townhouse"
developments, etc. These communities typically have between a dozen and a
few thousand members. They are typically constructed to provide minimal
utility services (i.e. roadways, etc) to residents, and to enforce very
minimal regulations on behavior, which almost exclusively bear on citizen
interactions (a typical instance being a rule against making too much
noise at night, or against leaving garbage in commons areas). These
communities have not been observed to get very large in spite of opportunities
for the construction of extremely large communities by major developers --
at most, they tend to be a couple of miles accross, and they typically
have weaker rules the larger they get. Experience has especially shown that
fascist rules tend not to be generated in such communities -- as an example,
attempts by many coops in New York to start "flip taxes" on apartment sales
have usually resulted in the elimination of the board that implemented
the concept following a rapid plunge in home resale values.

2. Theoretical considerations
One must also ask the question "how could a private community grow to be
the size of a country?"
Private communities, as I've noted, tend to be small and tend to be structured
around a group of homes or businesses with a tightly bound set of mutual
interests -- i.e. a condominium apartment building or an indoor shopping
mall. Presumably, of course, it would be possible for private communities
to start out larger or to grow. However, it seems rather unlikely that
communities would start out much larger than the size I've mentioned --
certainly its rather difficult, in the presense of holdout and other
considerations, to imagine how a private community could start out in an
existing town of considerable size, let alone a major city or whole country.
People must have reasonably communalized interest (such as the maintainance
of a common access road and common utilities like sewers) in order to
have a reason to form a private community -- it is hard to see what benefit
one would gain from being part of a private community that maintained
roads fifty or one hundred miles away, or even what would inspire people
to unanimously join such a structure in the first place. Similarly,
I would not be able to imagine what would inspire the members of a private
community, which is essentially a mutual company, to merge with another unless
there were extremely clear benefits -- such mergers likely doing no more
than joining a pair of adjacent apartment buildings, prehaps. I would also
like to emphasize once again the costs that a private community would
face in setting up draconian regulations -- lowered property values,
community member resentment against being searched for drugs twice a day,
costs for guards, etc, all of which would have to be imposed on the community
by its members. Provided the community remains fairly small and operates
in a competitive environment, its hard to believe that such actions would
be common.

In general, the absense of a clear incentive to form large communities, 
coupled with the problems that such communities would face, the problems 
associated with starting draconian community regulations, and the 
assumed general widespread desire for a low-interference society that we 
must assume would have to be present for a PPL environment to be present 
in the first place, all make me tend to believe that its unlikely that 
private communities operating in a PPL structure would form large draconian 
pseudostates that would regulate "immigration" or personal drug use.

I must emphasize that I have no proof for this position, but of course
there is no way to prove such a thing short of trying it.

Perry

