Date: Tue, 9 Feb 93 14:01:18 EST
From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: This Too Shall Pass...

> From: Duncan Frissell <76630.3577@compuserve.com>
> 
> 8)  There is an over emphasis on government's ability to shoot people.  If
> any nation's existence as a nation depends solely on its ability to shoot
> people, it has already lost.  The nation state today will not be facing
> a conspiracy or organized opposition of any kind.  It will be facing 
> billions of people from all around the earth building alternative 
> institutions and ways of living outside of its control.  The nation state
> was invented about 1600 AD.  It is not written in stone.  The Church used
> to be a major power center in Europe.  It no longer is.  That revolution
> occurred with little bloodshed.

Here I must disagree with Duncan's historical facts.

The nation-state long predates 1600AD, although several modern European
states started up back then. Certainly the Roman Empire was a huge state.
Certainly England was a nation-state as early as 1066AD. Certainly China
has been run and organized on nation-state lines for nearly 2000 years.

The church's hold on power was not broken bloodlessly, either. The Thirty
Years War, which ended with the Treaty of Westphalia in 1648, was one
of the bloodiest messes on record. The Thirty Years War was essentially
about whether the Catholic Church was to continue to rule Europe. It lost --
one of the major conditions of the treaty was the subordination of national
established religions to the power of the local state.

Overall, I feel that Duncan is prehaps overly optimistic. Although I agree
that networks and modern technological revolutions are going to undermine
the state, I don't think its going to be by simple absorbtion of the
traditions of our anarchic networks, and I don't think that the state
will merely fade away bloodlessly.

Perry

