Date: Tue, 9 Feb 93 14:10:35 PST
From: ghsvax!hal@uunet.UU.NET (Hal Finney)
Subject: Econ: Zero-sum games

Robin writes:

> Let us assume the standard CAPM finance model.
> [...]
> Now a bet is basically a way to split an asset into two or more state
> contingent assets.  So if you bet by splitting a riskless asset, the
> same warning applies.  You may be implicitly betting against progress.
> But bets need not split riskless assets - they can instead split as
> high a beta asset is liquidly available.  In general derivative assets
> need not have low average beta.  
> [...]
> In theory, the only risk that the market
> will/should pay you for is beta.  The rest you can eliminate with
> enough diversification.  Diversify your bets.

It still seems to me that the risk of a bet which is denominated in
some security is greater than the risk of just holding that security.
You face two risks in the betting case: one is the risk that the
security may lose value, and the other is the risk of losing your bet.

Now, Robin suggests that diversification is the key to eliminating the
second risk.  One must ask, then, why bet at all?  If you take so many
betting positions that you'll win as many as you lose, you may have
diversified away your betting risk, but you've also diversified away
any betting return.  You could have just held the underlying security
if that was your goal.

But there is a more fundamental problem in applying CAPM to betting
markets.  (This analysis is based on one of the books I got at the
library.)  What is maximal diversification in a betting market?  It is
to hold both sides of the bet!  And sure enough, if you do that, you
will find that your return and risk really are just the same as that
of the underlying security.  This is a "proof" that diversification
can eliminate betting risk.

Does this seem silly?  It is exactly what CAPM predicts (according to
my reference).  CAPM says that investors will end up holding all
assets in proportion to their availability.  Since by construction
equal numbers of "long" and "short" positions exist in betting
markets, it follows from CAPM that all investors hold equal numbers of
both positions.  Again, we have that betters will hold both sides of
each bet.

This means, I think, that diversification in the sense used in CAPM
doesn't really apply to a betting market.  If you diversify your beta
down to the beta of the underlying securities, you will in effect be
betting against yourself and not holding a position in the betting
market.

It follows, then, that if you do bet, you are taking an additional
risk, and I still don't see how the betting market can compensate you
for that risk.

Hal

P.S. According to my source, one reason why CAPM doesn't work for such
markets is that it assumes that the quantity of each available asset
is fixed.  But the number of betting contracts is highly elastic, as
can be seen by looking at the variations in open interest in any
futures contract.  Bets are not like stocks.  New pairs of bets can be
created without limit, without hurting the value of existing bets.

